.3L1389.1 68L1407.7 24.6L1444.5 -56.6L1462.8 -107.8L1487.5 -168.4" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="6"/><path d="M1117.7 637.3L1317.7 567.3" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="5"/><circle cx="1117.7" cy="637.3" r="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="4"/><text class="lb sb" x="1327.7" y="557.3">Setback line</text><path d="M847.1 1244.3L1077.1 1304.3" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="5"/><circle cx="847.1" cy="1244.3" r="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-width="4"/><text class="lb" x="1087.1" y="1344.3">Dune crest</text><text class="lb" x="1087.1" y="1404.3">(baseline)</text></svg></div><ul class="c3-pi-legend"><li><i style="background:rgba(196,120,58,.75);border:2px solid #ffb35c"></i>Part of the house is past the setback line (about 200 square feet or more)</li><li><i style="background:rgba(255,255,255,.6);border:2px solid #8a96a3"></i>The house is behind it</li></ul><figcaption>The south end of the island, just north of the south-end parking lot. Lines: the state's current baseline and setback line. Outlines: Georgetown County's 2024 building footprints. Aerial: USDA NAIP via The National Map (USGS), public domain. Map: Chapter3 Realty.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a house past the setback line, state law allows:</p><ul class="c3-pi-rules"><li><strong>Repair</strong>A house damaged by a storm, but not destroyed, can be repaired.</li><li><strong>Rebuild</strong>A house a storm destroys can be rebuilt only if it is no larger past the setback line, no wider along the beach and no farther seaward than before, moved landward where possible, and never past the baseline.</li><li><strong>New house</strong>A new house built partly past the setback line may have at most 5,000 square feet of heated space.</li><li><strong>The town's own line</strong>The town draws its own Shore Protection Line, which is usually stricter than the state's.</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Before you offer on an oceanfront house, we get the survey and the elevation certificate and see where both lines fall on the lot.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What the town lets you build</h3><ul class="c3-pi-rules"><li><strong>Height</strong>Up to 35 feet above the design flood elevation, which is 3 feet above FEMA's base flood elevation.</li><li><strong>Size</strong>Heated floor area between 2,000 and 4,000 square feet, and no more than 40 percent of the lot.</li><li><strong>Paving</strong>Roofs and paved areas together no more than 40 percent of the lot. Driveways are gravel, shell or crushed stone, never paved.</li><li><strong>Approval</strong>A new house needs the town's Architectural Review Board. Taking down a protected tree needs a town permit.</li></ul><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The beach in front</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The town rebuilt its beach in 2019 and 2020, placing 1.1 million cubic yards of sand along 2.6 miles. Its planned funding was the town's beach fund, a state grant and a bank loan. The beach fund is the town's 3 percent local accommodations tax, which guests pay on rentals.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The south tip will change. Under a 2025 settlement with the Prince George community across the inlet, a new inlet is to be cut about 800 feet south of the south-end parking lot and kept inside a set zone. The town put $150,000 into the $4 million fund and gets the right to 100,000 cubic yards of sand for future beach work.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="hurricanes"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Hurricanes">How often do hurricanes hit Pawleys Island?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Since 1950, 44 storms have passed within 50 miles of the middle of the island, and 7 of them were hurricanes at their closest point. Ian, in 2022, passed about 5 miles away. Matthew, in 2016, and Charley, in 2004, came closer still.</p><figure class="c3-gchart c3-pi-fig"><span class="cap" style="display:block;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1.1em;line-height:1.35;margin:0 0 .5rem">Storms that passed within 50 miles of the island, by decade</span><svg class="c3-pi-cols" viewBox="0 0 360 236" width="100%" role="img" aria-label="Column chart of storms whose center passed within 50 miles of Pawleys Island, by decade: 1950s 5 storms, 2 hurricanes; 1960s 5 storms, 0 hurricanes; 1970s 2 storms, 0 hurricanes; 1980s 4 storms, 0 hurricanes; 1990s 4 storms, 0 hurricanes; 2000s 8 storms, 1 hurricanes; 2010s 8 storms, 2 hurricanes; 2020s 8 storms, 2 hurricanes."><line x1="34" x2="352" y1="196" y2="196" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".35"/><text x="28" y="201" text-anchor="end">0</text><line x1="34" x2="352" y1="109" y2="109" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".12"/><text x="28" y="114" text-anchor="end">5</text><line x1="34" x2="352" y1="22" y2="22" stroke="#1c2028" stroke-opacity=".12"/><text x="28" y="27" text-anchor="end">10</text><rect x="40.4" y="109" width="27" height="87" rx="3" fill="#d9b48f"/><rect x="40.4" y="161.2" width="27" height="34.8" rx="3" fill="#c4783a"/><text class="v" x="53.9" y="103" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text x="53.9" y="218" text-anchor="middle">’50s</text><rect x="80.1" y="109" width="27" height="87" rx="3" fill="#d9b48f"/><text class="v" x="93.6" y="103" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text x="93.6" y="218" text-anchor="middle">’60s</text><rect x="119.9" y="161.2" width="27" height="34.8" rx="3" fill="#d9b48f"/><text class="v" x="133.4" y="155.2" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text x="133.4" y="218" text-anchor="middle">’70s</text><rect x="159.6" y="126.4" width="27" height="69.6" rx="3" fill="#d9b48f"/><text class="v" x="173.1" y="120.4" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="173.1" y="218" text-anchor="middle">’80s</text><rect x="199.4" y="126.4" width="27" height="69.6" rx="3" fill="#d9b48f"/><text class="v" x="212.9" y="120.4" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="212.9" y="218" text-anchor="middle">’90s</text><rect x="239.1" y="56.8" width="27" height="139.2" rx="3" fill="#d9b48f"/><rect x="239.1" y="178.6" width="27" height="17.4" rx="3" fill="#c4783a"/><text class="v" x="252.6" y="50.8" text-anchor="middle">8</text><text x="252.6" y="218" text-anchor="middle">’00s</text><rect x="278.9" y="56.8" width="27" height="139.2" rx="3" fill="#d9b48f"/><rect x="278.9" y="161.2" width="27" height="34.8" rx="3" fill="#c4783a"/><text class="v" x="292.4" y="50.8" text-anchor="middle">8</text><text x="292.4" y="218" text-anchor="middle">’10s</text><rect x="318.6" y="56.8" width="27" height="139.2" rx="3" fill="#d9b48f"/><rect x="318.6" y="161.2" width="27" height="34.8" rx="3" fill="#c4783a"/><text class="v" x="332.1" y="50.8" text-anchor="middle">8</text><text x="332.1" y="218" text-anchor="middle">’20s</text></svg><ul class="c3-pi-names" aria-label="The seven hurricanes"><li>Hazel, 1954</li><li>Helene, 1958</li><li>Charley, 2004</li><li>Matthew, 2016</li><li>Dorian, 2019</li><li>Isaias, 2020</li><li>Ian, 2022</li></ul><figcaption>Darker: storms that were hurricanes at their closest point. NOAA National Hurricane Center storm tracks (HURDAT2), 1950 to 2025, measured from the middle of the island.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Hurricane Hazel, on October 15, 1954, washed away most of the houses built on the south end after World War II. The older houses came through it, as they had come through many storms before.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="taxes"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Property taxes">What are property taxes on Pawleys Island?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">It depends most on whether the house is your legal residence. On the island's middle price, about $2.45 million, the county's current estimate is about $11,200 a year as a legal residence and about $34,900 as a second home or rental, about 3 times as much.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">South Carolina taxes a legal residence on 4 percent of its value and a second home or rental on 6 percent, and the tax rate on a second home is about twice as high: 237.1 mills against 114.0. A mill is $1 of tax for each $1,000 of that 4 or 6 percent.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">House price</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Legal residence, a year</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Second home or rental, a year</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$580,000, the middle west of US 17</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$2,740</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$8,350</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$900,000</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$4,150</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$12,850</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$2,450,000, the island's middle</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$11,220</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$34,900</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Each figure is Georgetown County's own estimate: the price times 4 or 6 percent, times the mills, plus the fees on the bill. Those are $44 on the island and $96 in the mainland district west of US 17, its storm water and household fees together. The county taxes its own appraised value, which can differ from the price.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Town of Pawleys Island levies no property tax of its own, so the island pays the same rates as the mainland district around it. The town's two largest revenue lines are the state accommodations tax and insurance license fees.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Worked example: the island's middle house, not your main home</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Take an island house at the middle price, about $2.45 million, that you keep as a second home or rent out. Before the mortgage, it costs about $39,700 a year in property tax and federal flood insurance, and more for wind and homeowners insurance.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Each year</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">About</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Property tax at the 6 percent rate, with the $44 in fees</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$34,900</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">A federal flood policy, the middle for an island house that is not the owner's main home</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$4,770</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Together</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$39,700</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Wind and homeowners insurance</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">By house</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="renting"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Renting it out">Can you rent out a house on Pawleys Island?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. Short-term rentals are allowed on the island. Guests pay about 11 percent in lodging taxes on stays under 90 days: 8 percent collected by the state and the town's 3 percent local accommodations tax, which the owner files with the town each month.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the mainland, a homeowners association can limit rentals in its own papers. We read them before you offer. The rules by town are on our <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">short-term rental rules page</a>, and what a rental earns in each town is on our <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Airbnb income page</a>.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For a yearly tenant instead, see our <a href="/invest/long-term-rental/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">long-term rental guide</a>. If you are selling a rental elsewhere to buy here, a <a href="/invest/strategies/1031-exchange/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">1031 exchange</a> can put off the tax on the sale.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="living"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Living there">What is it like to live on Pawleys Island?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Quiet most of the year. On Census Day 2020, in April, 69 percent of the island's homes were seasonal or vacation homes, and 130 people lived on the island. In the Litchfield Beach Census area, north of the island, the seasonal share was 31 percent. The town still uses its old nickname, "arrogantly shabby".</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Houses taxed as someone's legal residence, by part"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Houses taxed as someone's legal residence, by part</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Part</span><span role="columnheader">Share of houses</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Pawleys Island, the town</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.206" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">About 1 in 5</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Litchfield, ocean side of US 17</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.414" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">About 4 in 10</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Mainland behind the island</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.733" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">About 3 in 4</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">West of US 17</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.857" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">About 6 in 7</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>South Carolina taxes a house its owner lives in as a legal residence at 4 percent of its value; the rest are second homes and rentals at 6 percent. Georgetown County's 2026 tax records.</figcaption></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">A South Carolina beach</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The tax bills for 7 in 10 island houses go to South Carolina addresses: 127 to Pawleys Island itself, and most of the rest to Columbia, Greenville, Charleston, Georgetown, Mount Pleasant and Florence. Only 143 of 480 leave the state.</p><style>main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact{padding-top:1rem;padding-bottom:.85rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .cap{margin-bottom:.7rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.1rem .9rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowheader]{line-height:1.3}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .b{height:.5rem;min-width:.5rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact .v{line-height:1.25}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact figcaption{margin-top:.8rem}@media (max-width:699px){main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=rowgroup]{gap:.45rem}main#main .c3-gchart.c3-gchart-compact [role=row]{gap:.05rem}}</style><figure class="c3-gchart c3-gchart-compact"><div role="table" aria-label="Where the tax bills for island houses go"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Where the tax bills for island houses go</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">City</span><span role="columnheader">Houses</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Pawleys Island</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.888" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">127 houses</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Columbia</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.231" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">33 houses</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Greenville</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.189" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">27 houses</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Charleston</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.14" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">20 houses</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Georgetown</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.091" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">13 houses</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Mount Pleasant</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.084" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">12 houses</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Florence</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.07" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">10 houses</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Out of state</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">143 houses</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>The mailing city on each island house's tax bill, Georgetown County records. A mailing city is where the bill goes, not always where the owner lives. Out of state, North Carolina, Georgia, Tennessee and Virginia lead.</figcaption></figure><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">A normal week on the island</h3><ul class="c3-pi-rules"><li><strong>On the water</strong>Fishing in the surf and crabbing from the two marsh bridges. Three boat ramps on the island.</li><li><strong>The beach</strong>No lifeguards at any time. No cars, golf carts or e-bikes on the sand, and no fireworks anywhere on the island.</li><li><strong>Parking</strong>Free. The south-end lot is the largest free beach lot in Georgetown County, and 8 other public accesses add about 141 spaces.</li><li><strong>Dogs</strong>On the beach all year. From May 1 to September 30, 8 a.m. to 8 p.m., on a leash, even in the water.</li><li><strong>Golf carts</strong>On island roads with a state registration and a town sticker, an owner's address within 2 miles of the town limits, and a licensed driver 16 or older. Not at night or in the rain.</li><li><strong>Driving</strong>A 25 mph speed limit across the island. Low-speed vehicles are not allowed on town roads.</li></ul><style>.c3ph-f{position:relative;margin:0;border-radius:6px;overflow:hidden;background:var(--ivory-2)}.c3ph-f img{display:block;width:100%;height:auto;object-fit:cover}.c3ph-cr{position:absolute;right:0;bottom:0;max-width:100%;padding:1.6rem .6rem .35rem 2.6rem;font:400 .625rem/1.3 var(--sans);letter-spacing:.01em;color:#fff;text-align:right;background:radial-gradient(farthest-side at 100% 100%,rgba(28,32,40,.72),rgba(28,32,40,.4) 55%,rgba(28,32,40,0));white-space:nowrap;overflow:hidden;text-overflow:ellipsis;pointer-events:none}.c3ph-cr a{color:#fff;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(255,255,255,.5);text-underline-offset:2px;pointer-events:auto;padding:10px 0 6px}.c3ph-hero{margin:1.6rem 0 2rem}.c3ph-hero .c3ph-f{border-radius:8px}.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:4/3}@media (min-width:700px){.c3ph-hero img{aspect-ratio:16/9}}.c3ph-hero figcaption{color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.55rem}</style><figure class="c3ph-hero" style="margin:1.4rem 0 1.6rem;max-width:640px;"><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/55-plus/seasons-at-prince-creek-west/pawleys-island-beach-walkway-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/55-plus/seasons-at-prince-creek-west/pawleys-island-beach-walkway-600w.webp 600w, /images/55-plus/seasons-at-prince-creek-west/pawleys-island-beach-walkway-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 640px" width="1200" height="900" alt="A wooden walkway over green dunes leading to the ocean on Pawleys Island" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: Phoebe. License: CC BY-SA 4.0" title="License: CC BY-SA 4.0">Photo: Phoebe</a></span></div><figcaption>A wooden walkway from a house over the dunes to the beach on Pawleys Island.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Schools are assigned by address in the Georgetown County School District, and we look up the assigned schools for any house you like. The town has its own police department.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How far is everything?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Drive times by car from Town Hall on the island, without traffic. Summer traffic on US 17 adds time.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">From Town Hall to</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">By car, about</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Huntington Beach State Park</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">15 min</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Tidelands Waccamaw Community Hospital</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">20 min</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Brookgreen Gardens</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">20 min</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Downtown Georgetown</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">20 min</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Myrtle Beach International Airport</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">40 min</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Broadway at the Beach, Myrtle Beach</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">40 min</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Downtown Charleston</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">1 hr 45 min</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="history"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The old houses">Why do the old Pawleys Island houses look the way they do?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">They were built as summer houses for rice planters' families, who came from May to November to escape the malaria of the rice fields. Enslaved people carried the households to the island each May, and enslaved workers built the older of the two causeways under Governor R. F. W. Allston, a rice planter.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Cypress was the building material used most often. It was hewn on the mainland, and each timber was cut with Roman numerals so the house could be put together on the island. Most of the old houses stand on high brick foundations, which keep them above ordinary storm tides and let air move under the floor.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Pawleys Island Historic District, on the National Register of Historic Places since 1972, covers the middle of the island, about 8,400 by 1,400 feet.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">The town's historic rules</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Since 2026, under the town's Ordinance 2026-02, the whole island is a local historic district. A house built in 1954 or earlier can be placed on the town's list of historic properties: the owner, a council member or a board member can nominate it, and the council decides.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A listed house needs a certificate of appropriateness for outside changes and for demolition, the board can delay a demolition for up to 180 days, and the listing binds later owners. No permit issues while a nomination is pending. A house not on the list has none of these rules. We check whether a house is listed or nominated before you offer.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="pawleys-island-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Pawleys Island FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What county is Pawleys Island in?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Georgetown County. The island and the mainland around it are all in Georgetown County, not Horry County.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is Pawleys Island part of Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">No. The island is its own town, and the mainland around it is unincorporated Georgetown County. Broadway at the Beach in Myrtle Beach is about 40 minutes away by car.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is the ZIP code for Pawleys Island?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">29585, for the island and most of the mainland around it. A Pawleys Island address does not mean a house on the island.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How many people live on Pawleys Island?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Census counted 130 people in the town in 2020. Most of its homes were seasonal or vacation homes.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is Pawleys Island expensive?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">On the island, yes: a house sold for about $2.45 million at the middle. On the mainland, a house sold for about $580,000 to $675,000.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is Pawleys Island safe?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We do not rate any place as safe or unsafe. The town has its own police department.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What schools serve Pawleys Island?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The Georgetown County School District, by address. We look up the assigned schools for any house you like.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you drive a golf cart on Pawleys Island?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, on island roads, with a state registration and a town sticker, a licensed driver 16 or older, and not at night or in the rain.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://gis1.georgetowncountysc.org/portal/rest/services" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Georgetown County GIS</a>, <a href="https://www.gtcountysc.gov/385/Property-Tax-Calculator" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">county tax calculator</a>, <a href="https://data.census.gov/profile/Pawleys_Island_town,_South_Carolina?g=160XX00US4555015" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Census 2020</a>, <a href="https://www.fema.gov/openfema-data-page/fima-nfip-redacted-policies-v2" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">OpenFEMA policies</a>, <a href="https://www.nhc.noaa.gov/data/#hurdat" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">NOAA HURDAT2</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t48c039.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 48-39</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43</a>, <a href="https://dor.sc.gov/sites/dor/files/forms/ST575.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SCDOR ST-575</a>, <a href="https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/72001211" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Register</a>, <a href="https://www.townofpawleysisland.com/about-pawleys-island/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Town of Pawleys Island</a>, <a href="https://www.townofpawleysisland.com/flood-information/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">flood information</a>, <a href="https://www.townofpawleysisland.com/passed-ordinances/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">passed ordinances</a>, <a href="https://www.townofpawleysisland.com/beach-renourishment-faqs/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">renourishment</a>, <a href="https://www.townofpawleysisland.com/2025-legal-settlement-faqs/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">2025 settlement</a>, <a href="https://www.townofpawleysisland.com/beach-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">beach rules</a>. Our counts from county parcels, 2024 footprints, flood zones and last deeds (houses; a market sale is $10,000 or more). Listings counted in aggregate from CCMLS. Drives without traffic.</p><style>.c3ph-credits{margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding:1rem 0 0;border-top:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted);font-size:.8rem;line-height:1.55;max-width:760px}.c3ph-credits .c3ph-h{font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 .4rem}.c3ph-credits [role=listitem]{margin:0 0 .3rem}.c3ph-credits a{color:inherit;text-decoration:underline;text-decoration-color:rgba(28,32,40,.3);text-underline-offset:2px}</style><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Pawleys Island at the North Causeway from above: the salt marsh, the causeway, and the rows of houses between the marsh and the beach. Photo: USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP, <a href="https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://imagery.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSNAIPImagery/ImageServer" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The National Map (USGS)</a>. Chapter3 Realty cut this view from the NAIP service and added labels placed from map data.</div><div role="listitem">A walkway over the dunes on Pawleys Island. Photo: Phoebe, <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>, via <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pawleys_beach_walkway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wikimedia Commons</a>.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them. Each photo keeps the license listed with it.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-pawleys-island/pawleys-island-at-the-north-causeway-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/submarkets-pawleys-island/pawleys-island-at-the-north-causeway-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":900,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Pawleys Island at the North Causeway from above: the salt marsh, the causeway, and the rows of houses between the marsh and the beach","caption":"Pawleys Island at the North Causeway from above: the salt marsh, the causeway, and the rows of houses between the marsh and the beach.","description":"Aerial photo of Pawleys Island, labelled: the North Causeway crossing the salt marsh from the mainland to the island, a narrow strip of houses, and the Atlantic Ocean","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP"},"creditText":"USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP, public domain, via The National Map (USGS)","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (USDA Farm Service Agency, NAIP)","license":"https://www.fsa.usda.gov/help/policies-and-links/","acquireLicensePage":"https://imagery.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSNAIPImagery/ImageServer","isBasedOn":"https://imagery.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/USGSNAIPImagery/ImageServer","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"The North Causeway and the middle of the island, Pawleys Island, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true},{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/55-plus/seasons-at-prince-creek-west/pawleys-island-beach-walkway-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/55-plus/seasons-at-prince-creek-west/pawleys-island-beach-walkway-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":900,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"A wooden walkway from a house over the dunes to the beach on Pawleys Island","caption":"A wooden walkway from a house over the dunes to the beach on Pawleys Island.","description":"A wooden walkway over green dunes leading to the ocean on Pawleys Island","creator":{"@type":"Person","name":"Phoebe"},"creditText":"Phoebe, CC BY-SA 4.0, via Wikimedia Commons","copyrightNotice":"© Phoebe, CC BY-SA 4.0","license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pawleys_beach_walkway.jpg","isBasedOn":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pawleys_beach_walkway.jpg","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"Pawleys Island, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--ivory);margin-bottom:1rem">Island or mainland, we check four things before you offer.</h2><p style="color:rgba(244,239,232,.75);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:1.6rem">The flood zone and the house's elevation certificate, where the state's setback line falls on an oceanfront lot, whether the town lists the house as historic, and the tax bill as a legal residence or a second home.</p><a class="btn btn-brass btn-lg" href="/contact/?place=Pawleys%20Island">Talk to a Pawleys Island agent</a></div></section></main>
