+A food map is typically issued following a food risk assessment conducted 
+in connection with a community’s conversion from the NFIP's Emergency 
+Program to the NFIP’s Regular Program. If a detailed assessment, termed 
+an FIS, has been performed, the food map will show insurance risk zones, 
+foodplain boundaries, and in some cases Base Flood Elevations (BFEs). 
+The food map may also show a delineation of the regulatory foodway. 
+Actuarial rates, based on the risk zone designations shown on the food 
+map, are then applied for newly constructed, substantially improved, and 
